 "answer": {
  "answer": "The goal was not met. Only two settings, on two different scenes, were confirmed as failing most of the time, against the five the study asked for. Neither one is a clean case of the policy rear-ending a braking lead car. On 12855a41 with the lead car at 0.5x speed and no time shift (S18), every run made front contact with lead car 5 only 0.5 s after the policy took over. In each run the ego was still exactly on the recorded human path, so the contact may come from the replayed setup or the box sizes rather than from the policy. On 096988dd with the lead car shifted 1.5 s later and at 0.5x speed (S6), every run failed, and the policy was at fault. But in each run the ego drifted left into the next lane and hit a pickup there (vehicle 41); it did not hit the lead car. The other six scenes produced no failures at any setting tried. Most of those settings were run only once or twice, so their passes are hints rather than evidence that they are safe.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "On 12855a41, slowing the lead car to 0.5x with no time shift failed every run. In each run the ego made front contact with lead car 5 just 0.5 s after handoff, while still exactly on the recorded human path. The triage judged the policy's fault unclear in two runs and not the policy's in one (a likely box or track mismatch). This confirmed case may be an artifact of the replayed setup rather than a rear-end by the policy.",
    "settings": [
     "S18"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "On the same scene with the lead car shifted 1 s later at 0.5x, the runs passed. The S18 contact therefore depends on the exact timing of the replayed lead car at handoff.",
    "settings": [
